#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:5
#: c37cebc461c84b0d908316e4fe8797bc
msgid "GPIOs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:7
#: b9c7545bc6b147fb9effc1456d7ef415
msgid "In order to use an output as a GPIO, the individual ``SERVOx_FUNCTION`` parameter is merely set to \"-1\". If set to \"0\", it remains a PWM output, unassigned to a function, but will not output any PWM signal unless commanded by scripting or a GCS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:9
#: 99756fdc3c114053aa082b9e0cb56938
msgid "If the servo function is being \"mirrored\" to a remote device, as in the case of a DroneCAN or KDECAN ESC, then in order to change the autopilot board's corresponding output pin to be a GPIO, but allow the ``SERVOx_FUNCTION`` to still be assigned to the remote device, the :ref:`SERVO_GPIO_MASK<SERVO_GPIO_MASK>` parameter can be used to assign the board pin to be a GPIO without affecting the ``SERVOx_FUNCTION`` assignment for the remote device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:12
#: 4eecf5e4b8094d83880d388374f2325d
msgid "GPIOs should only source or sink 8mA (20mA absolute maximum). They are intended as logic-level IO and must not be used to directly drive relay coils, motors, LED strings, etc. Directly connecting electromechanical or high-current devices to GPIOs can permanently damage the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:14
#: 47fe9b89ed8a4dd6990598e21900c0ee
msgid "General Purpose Input/Outputs (GPIOs) are used in ArduPilot for control of :ref:`relays<common-relay>`, actuators, LEDs, :ref:`camera triggers<common-camera-shutter-with-servo>`, :ref:`Start Button<startstop-switch>` etc. Some functions also use a GPIO pin as an input, like :ref:`common-rpm`. Some autopilots provide dedicated GPIO pins (sometimes labeled \"CAPTURE\" pins). In addition, GPIOs can be obtained by re-configuring the PWM outputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:17
#: c0735764610a4e84bf8549744f260c8a
msgid "Configuring GPIOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:18
#: 73c8c693a4c84d2b81119d41c2b77aac
msgid "In order to set a PWM/SERVO/MOTOR output to be a GPIO function, the individual ``SERVOx_FUNCTION`` parameter is set to \"-1\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:20
#: 689661628c804f6a984b6995aa141d2c
msgid "for autopilots using IOMCUs, if a \"MAIN\" output is configured as a GPIO, it can only function as an output (ie RELAY,etc.) not an input. \"AUX\" outputs can function either as inputs or outputs when configures as a GPIO."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:22
#: b2efdb76c0ba437db54f7d74af3779f7
msgid "Every time the autopilot initializes, it sends a log message to the ground control station, showing which outputs are PWM/Oneshot/or DShot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:28
#: c1bf3ec14def4b0181110cac05a69d62
msgid "GPIO \"PIN\" NUMBER"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:30
#: 7d8aa9c0e0d746c883b462ee85058155
msgid "Some GPIO-based functions require that the GPIO \"pin number\" to be entered into an associated parameter. This pin number is assigned in the autopilot's hardware definition file. Usually, the first GPIO capable output is assigned pin 50, the second 51, etc. So in the above case of the Pixhawk, AUX OUT 6 is pin 55."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:32
#: 2a3fc3f995b94cc597867e4a72ba8391
msgid "You can verify an output's GPIO pin number assignment easily. First, find its hwdef.dat file `here <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/libraries/AP_HAL_ChibiOS/hwdef>`__ and determine the GPIO pin number listed beside its output number, as shown below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:37
#: a1f3f11ed31a4e18842e5ae7e3b458a9
msgid "IOMCU \"MAIN\" output 1 thru 8 are mapped to GPIO pin numbers 101 to 108, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-gpios.rst:39
#: c23e81cb0dcc4371b23822f044e8c04d
msgid "Usually, changing any feature or function's GPIO pin assignment will require a reboot for it to take effect."
msgstr ""
